WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:06.986
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:12.872
- [Narrator] Grass is
something we take for granted.

00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:15.005
We walk all over it, lay on it,

00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:17.172
and sometimes mow it down.

00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:21.576
Most of us don't give it much thought.

00:00:21.576 --> 00:00:24.051
Few of us appreciate it
the way that Dan Harvey

00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:26.781
and Heather Ackroyd do .

00:00:26.781 --> 00:00:28.573
- I think we create these pieces partly

00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:32.925
because just love actually the
magic of seeing them in print

00:00:32.925 --> 00:00:35.059
and come to life and grow.

00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:37.960
The British artists blur the lines

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.093
between science, nature and art

00:00:40.093 --> 00:00:44.365
by manipulating the nature
processes that fuel life itself

00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:46.413
to create their canvases.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:48.717
- [Dan] You’re actually
imbuing it with this energy

00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:50.717
and creating with life.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.837
They're almost Day-Glo images
when you first see them.

00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:56.995
They are very, very beautiful.

00:00:56.995 --> 00:00:59.578
(upbeat music)

00:01:04.504 --> 00:01:06.381
- If you actually look at what's

00:01:06.381 --> 00:01:07.725
happened in the last 25 years,

00:01:07.725 --> 00:01:09.837
if you look at what's happened
in the last five years,

00:01:09.837 --> 00:01:11.245
mega flooding scenarios-

00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:12.696
- [Dan] Severe weather
events are happening time

00:01:12.696 --> 00:01:15.427
and time and time again and
increasingly more frequently.

00:01:15.427 --> 00:01:17.731
- And the science is very, very clear

00:01:17.731 --> 00:01:20.035
and unambiguous about this.

00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:22.509
So our work is very much embraced

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:25.752
around processes of change, around nature,

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:29.176
around biodiversity laws,
around climate change.

00:01:29.176 --> 00:01:31.213
From the very first time that Dan

00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:32.835
and I started to work together,

00:01:32.835 --> 00:01:36.077
it was within our sight,
it's not necessarily

00:01:36.077 --> 00:01:39.149
a direct action or activism.

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:42.477
Sometimes pieces are
very, very poetic.

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:45.060
(upbeat music)

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:57.763
We maybe only do one,

00:01:57.763 --> 00:02:00.920
one or two photosynthesis works a year.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.395
I think what's interesting is is that

00:02:03.395 --> 00:02:05.784
we're not working with lawn or with sod

00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:08.685
or with turf, we work with the seed.

00:02:08.685 --> 00:02:11.268
(upbeat music)

00:02:13.208 --> 00:02:14.232
- [Narrator] Dan and Heather germinate

00:02:14.232 --> 00:02:16.365
their seeds for about two weeks.

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:18.584
When ready, they're
rubbed onto the canvas,

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:21.517
held in place by water paste.

00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:23.277
- [Heather] We're not carving or sculpting

00:02:23.277 --> 00:02:25.277
in that more traditional sense.

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:26.861
We're growing.

00:02:26.861 --> 00:02:30.275
- [Dan] The image sort of
develops over the period of weeks.

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:34.200
To grow one of our pieces,
we grow the grass vertically,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.504
then we turn the whole
of the studio space here

00:02:36.504 --> 00:02:39.149
into a dark room and so the only light

00:02:39.149 --> 00:02:41.496
that the grass receives as it's growing

00:02:41.496 --> 00:02:44.696
is from the projected, negative image.

00:02:44.696 --> 00:02:47.085
So where the strongest
light hits the grass,

00:02:47.085 --> 00:02:50.157
it produces more the chlorophyll,
more of the green pigment.

00:02:50.157 --> 00:02:52.376
Where there's less light, it's less green.

00:02:52.376 --> 00:02:54.168
Where's there's no light, it grows,

00:02:54.168 --> 00:02:56.131
but it's intonated and yellow.

00:02:56.131 --> 00:02:58.605
So you get the equivalent to
a black and white photograph

00:02:58.605 --> 00:03:00.995
but in terms of green to yellow.

00:03:00.995 --> 00:03:03.043
- [Heather] Portraits we tend to do around

00:03:03.043 --> 00:03:04.637
six foot by four foot or maybe seven foot

00:03:04.637 --> 00:03:06.029
by five foot in this case.

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:07.693
We can work really, really large scale.

00:03:07.693 --> 00:03:09.357
What's great about that is that it's not,

00:03:09.357 --> 00:03:12.088
it's not sized in a gratuitous way.

00:03:12.088 --> 00:03:15.096
Actually the resolution we get is really,

00:03:15.096 --> 00:03:17.075
really extraordinary, it's phenomenal.

00:03:17.075 --> 00:03:20.109
If you equate a chlorophyll
molecule with a pixel,

00:03:20.109 --> 00:03:22.157
then we're getting kind
of like many more pixels

00:03:22.157 --> 00:03:24.657
per kinda like square foot.

00:03:25.875 --> 00:03:28.813
- The detail you can get on the large
ones is absolutely fantastic.

00:03:28.813 --> 00:03:30.691
- [Heather] It's part of
a process of discovery.

00:03:30.691 --> 00:03:32.776
As artists you're looking
to make discoveries

00:03:32.776 --> 00:03:35.128
within the materials and
mediums that you work with.

00:03:35.128 --> 00:03:35.961
And light,

00:03:36.835 --> 00:03:38.200
and seeds,

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.395
water,

00:03:39.395 --> 00:03:41.187
crystals, all of those are kinda of part

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.661
of our palette of materials
that we play with.

00:03:43.661 --> 00:03:45.256
- [Narrator] If watered regularly

00:03:45.256 --> 00:03:46.989
and kept at low light levels,

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:50.136
these grass portraits
can last indefinitely.

00:03:50.136 --> 00:03:52.109
- [Dan] There's something
about seeing the way

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:54.243
that they grow and they
sort of materialize

00:03:54.243 --> 00:03:56.205
in front of you really and,

00:03:56.205 --> 00:03:58.851
you don't really get to see
them until the last moment

00:03:58.851 --> 00:04:01.325
really when you can put the lights up.

00:04:01.325 --> 00:04:03.908
(upbeat music)

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:08.900
- Oh wow.

00:04:10.637 --> 00:04:11.637
Grass me up.

00:04:13.229 --> 00:04:16.301
(laughter)

00:04:16.301 --> 00:04:21.165
- I feel that this piece has
got a real strength about it.

00:04:21.165 --> 00:04:25.347
I think the person we
photograph and their youth

00:04:25.347 --> 00:04:27.992
just has this real kinda connection

00:04:27.992 --> 00:04:29.613
with the camera and
he's somebody who cares

00:04:29.613 --> 00:04:32.530
passionately about what he's doing.

00:04:33.504 --> 00:04:36.440
It’s like a watercolor, the color
grazes through it you know.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.147
That's very, very beautiful.

00:04:38.147 --> 00:04:41.475
- I'm still a bit taken
aback by how it's grass. 

00:04:41.475 --> 00:04:43.267
- [Heather] I know, I know.

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:44.803
We're really, really pleased with it.

00:04:44.803 --> 00:04:48.970
We're really pleased with
the way that it's grown.

